{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-73856" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the elliptic paraboloid $z=\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{4}.$ Which of the following gives its axis of symmetry, written as an intersection of two planes?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-73856" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we write our equation as \n\n$$\nz-0=\\frac{(x-(-1))^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{4}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an elliptic paraboloid oriented along the $z$-axis that has its vertex at $(-1,2,0).$\n Since it is oriented along the $z$-axis, we conclude that its axis of symmetry is parallel to the $z$-axis.\n\nThe axis of symmetry should pass through the vertex of the paraboloid. Therefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry can be written as an intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-1 \\\\ y=2\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-73856" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "908a1ded9102a559411c5aaef5ec93dd5c34a8a0a53ff518afe5707837569617"} {"question" "q-73856" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-73856" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\begin{aligned}x=-1 \\\\ y=2\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}x=-1 \\\\ z=0\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ y=-2\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ y=2\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=0\\end{aligned}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-73856" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\begin{aligned}x=-1 \\\\ y=2\\end{aligned}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-164270" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the elliptic paraboloid $y+1=\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{5}+\\frac{(z-2)^{2}}{3}.$ Which of the following gives its axis of symmetry, written as an intersection of two planes?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-164270" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we write our equation as \n\n$$\ny-(-1)=\\frac{(x-(-4))^{2}}{5}+\\frac{(z-2)^{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an elliptic paraboloid oriented along the $y$-axis that has its vertex at $(-4,-1,2).$\n Since it is oriented along the $y$-axis, we conclude that its axis of symmetry is parallel to the $y$-axis.\n\nThe axis of symmetry should pass through the vertex of the paraboloid. Therefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry can be written as an intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-4 \\\\ z=2\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-164270" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "908a1ded9102a559411c5aaef5ec93dd5c34a8a0a53ff518afe5707837569617"} {"question" "q-164270" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-164270" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\begin{aligned}x=-4 \\\\ y=-1\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}x=-4 \\\\ z=2\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}x=4 \\\\ y=2\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}x=4 \\\\ z=-1\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}y=-1 \\\\ z=2\\end{aligned}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-164270" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\begin{aligned}x=-4 \\\\ z=2\\end{aligned}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-164265" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the vertex of the elliptic paraboloid $3z+6=3(x-3)^{2}+(y+2)^{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-164265" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The general equation of an elliptic paraboloid oriented along the $z$-axis with vertex at a point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ is \n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=\\frac{(x-{x}_{0})^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-{y}_{0})^{2}}{{b}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo find the vertex, we rewrite our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3z+6 & =3(x-3)^{2}+(y+2)^{2} \\\\ 3(z+2) & =3(x-3)^{2}+(y+2)^{2} \\\\ z+2 & =(x-3)^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}(y+2)^{2} \\\\ z-(-2) & =(x-3)^{2}+\\frac{(y-(-2))^{2}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an elliptic paraboloid that has its vertex at $(3,-2,-2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-164265" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "908a1ded9102a559411c5aaef5ec93dd5c34a8a0a53ff518afe5707837569617"} {"question" "q-164265" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-164265" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(-2,-2,3)"] ["math" "(2,2,3)"] ["math" "(2,3,-2)"] ["math" "(3,-2,-2)"] ["math" "(3,-2,-6)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-164265" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(3,-2,-2)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-164269" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the elliptic paraboloid $x=\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(z+1)^{2}}{6}.$ Which of the following gives its axis of symmetry, written as an intersection of two planes?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-164269" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we write our equation as \n\n$$\nx-0=\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(z-(-1))^{2}}{6}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an elliptic paraboloid oriented along the $x$-axis that has its vertex at $(0,3,-1).$\n Since it is oriented along the $x$-axis, we conclude that its axis of symmetry is parallel to the $x$-axis.\n\nThe axis of symmetry should pass through the vertex of the paraboloid. Therefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry can be written as an intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=3 \\\\ z=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-164269" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "908a1ded9102a559411c5aaef5ec93dd5c34a8a0a53ff518afe5707837569617"} {"question" "q-164269" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-164269" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=3\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}x=3 \\\\ y=1\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}y=0 \\\\ z=3\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}y=3 \\\\ z=-1\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}y=3 \\\\ z=1\\end{aligned}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-164269" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\begin{aligned}y=3 \\\\ z=-1\\end{aligned}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-72191" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the elliptic paraboloid $z+13=\\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{5}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{2}$, find its intercepts with the $y$-axis." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-72191" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the $y$-intercepts of the paraboloid, we substitute $x=0$ and $z=0$ into the equation of the paraboloid and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+13 & =\\frac{(0-5)^{2}}{5}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{2} \\\\ 13 & =\\frac{25}{5}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{2} \\\\ 13 & =5+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{2} & =8 \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =16 \\\\ y+1 & =\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the solutions $y=3,-5.$ \n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $y$-axis are $(0,-5,0)$ and $(0,3,0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-72191" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "908a1ded9102a559411c5aaef5ec93dd5c34a8a0a53ff518afe5707837569617"} {"question" "q-72191" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-72191" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$(0,-3,0)$ and $(0,5,0)$"] ["text" "$(0,-5,0)$ and $(0,3,0)$"] ["text" "$(0,-5,0)$ only"] ["text" "$(0,3,0)$ and $(0,5,0)$"] ["text" "$(0,3,0)$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-72191" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$(0,-5,0)$ and $(0,3,0)$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-73862" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the saddle point of the hyperbolic paraboloid $z-3=\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{4}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-73862" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The general equation of a hyperbolic paraboloid with a saddle point at $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ is \n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=\\frac{(x-{x}_{0})^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-{y}_{0})^{2}}{{b}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo find the saddle point, we rewrite our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-3 & =\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{4} \\\\ z-3 & =\\frac{(x-(-2))^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of a hyperbolic paraboloid with a saddle point at $(-2,4,3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-73862" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "908a1ded9102a559411c5aaef5ec93dd5c34a8a0a53ff518afe5707837569617"} {"question" "q-73862" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-73862" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(-2,4,0)"] ["math" "(-2,4,3)"] ["math" "(2,-4,-3)"] ["math" "(2,4,3)"] ["math" "(\\sqrt{2},2,1)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-73862" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(-2,4,3)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-72301" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the saddle point of the hyperbolic paraboloid $z+2=\\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{3}-\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-72301" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The general equation of a hyperbolic paraboloid with a saddle point at $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ is \n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=\\frac{(x-{x}_{0})^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-{y}_{0})^{2}}{{b}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo find the saddle point, we rewrite our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+2 & =\\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{3}-\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{2} \\\\ z-(-2) & =\\frac{(x-(-5))^{2}}{3}-\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of a hyperbolic paraboloid with a saddle point at $(-5,2,-2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-72301" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "908a1ded9102a559411c5aaef5ec93dd5c34a8a0a53ff518afe5707837569617"} {"question" "q-72301" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-72301" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(-2,5,-2)"] ["math" "(-5,-2,2)"] ["math" "(-5,2,-2)"] ["math" "(5,-2,-2)"] ["math" "(5,-2,2)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-72301" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(-5,2,-2)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-72305" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the hyperbolic paraboloid $(x+3)^{2}-2(y-3)^{2}=z-2$, find its intercepts with the $x$-axis." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-72305" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the $x$-intercepts of the paraboloid, we substitute $y=0$ and $z=0$ in the equation of the paraboloid:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2}-2(0-3)^{2} & =0-2 \\\\ (x+3)^{2}-18 & =-2 \\\\ (x+3)^{2} & =16 \\\\ x+3 & =\\pm 4 \\\\ x & =-3\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the solutions $x=-7$ and $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $x$-axis are $(-7,0,0)$ and $(1,0,0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-72305" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "908a1ded9102a559411c5aaef5ec93dd5c34a8a0a53ff518afe5707837569617"} {"question" "q-72305" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-72305" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(1,0,0)"] ["text" "$(-1,0,0)$ and $(7,0,0)$"] ["text" "$(-7,0,0)$ and $(-1,0,0)$"] ["text" "$(-7,0,0)$ and $(1,0,0)$"] ["text" "$(-7,0,0)$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-72305" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$(-7,0,0)$ and $(1,0,0)$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-72303" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the saddle point of the hyperbolic paraboloid $z-5=5(x-1)^{2}-3(y+4)^{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-72303" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The general equation of a hyperbolic paraboloid with a saddle point at $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ is \n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=\\frac{(x-{x}_{0})^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-{y}_{0})^{2}}{{b}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo find the saddle point, we rewrite our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-5 & =5(x-1)^{2}-3(y+4)^{2} \\\\ z-5 & =\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{1/5}-\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{1/3} \\\\ z-5 & =\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{1/5}-\\frac{(y-(-4))^{2}}{1/3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of a hyperbolic paraboloid with a saddle point at $(1,-4,5).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-72303" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "908a1ded9102a559411c5aaef5ec93dd5c34a8a0a53ff518afe5707837569617"} {"question" "q-72303" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-72303" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(-1,-4,5)"] ["math" "(-1,4,-5)"] ["math" "(-4,1,5)"] ["math" "(1,-4,-5)"] ["math" "(1,-4,5)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-72303" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(1,-4,5)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-164276" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the hyperbolic paraboloid $2(x-5)^{2}-3(y-1)^{2}=z+5$, find its intercepts with the $x$-axis." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-164276" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the $x$-intercepts of the paraboloid, we substitute $y=0$ and $z=0$ in the equation of the paraboloid:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(x-5)^{2}-3(0-1)^{2} & =0+5 \\\\ 2(x-5)^{2}-3 & =5 \\\\ 2(x-5)^{2} & =8 \\\\ (x-5)^{2} & =4 \\\\ x-5 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ x & =5\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the solutions $x=3$ and $x=7.$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $x$-axis are $(3,0,0)$ and $(7,0,0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-164276" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "908a1ded9102a559411c5aaef5ec93dd5c34a8a0a53ff518afe5707837569617"} {"question" "q-164276" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-164276" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$(-2,0,0)$ and $(2,0,0)$"] ["text" "$(-3,0,0)$ and $(2,0,0)$"] ["text" "$(3,0,0)$ and $(7,0,0)$"] ["text" "$(5,0,0)$ only"] ["text" "$(7,0,0)$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-164276" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$(3,0,0)$ and $(7,0,0)$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-35931" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the trace in the $yz$-plane of the elliptic paraboloid $z+1=\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-35931" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the trace in the $yz$-plane, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the paraboloid, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+1 & =\\frac{(0-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{3} \\\\ z+1 & =\\frac{4}{4}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{3} \\\\ z+1 & =1+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{3} \\\\ z & =\\frac{1}{3}(y-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives us the equation of the parabola in $yz$-plane." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-35931" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "908a1ded9102a559411c5aaef5ec93dd5c34a8a0a53ff518afe5707837569617"} {"question" "q-35931" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-35931" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "A hyperbola: ${x}^{2}-3(y-1)^{2}=1$"] ["text" "A parabola: $z=3(y-1)^{2}$"] ["text" "A parabola: $z=\\frac{1}{3}(y-1)^{2}$"] ["text" "Two intersecting lines: $y=\\pm (x-1)$"] ["text" "Two parallel lines: $y=(z\\pm 3)$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-35931" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "A parabola: $z=\\frac{1}{3}(y-1)^{2}$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-35577" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the trace in the $yz$-plane of the elliptic paraboloid $z=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{27}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-35577" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the trace in the $yz$-plane, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the paraboloid, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{{0}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{27} \\\\ z & =\\frac{1}{27}(y-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives us the equation of the parabola in $yz$-plane." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-35577" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "908a1ded9102a559411c5aaef5ec93dd5c34a8a0a53ff518afe5707837569617"} {"question" "q-35577" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-35577" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "A hyperbola: $(y-1)^{2}-{z}^{2}=1$"] ["text" "A parabola: $z=27(y-1)^{2}$"] ["text" "A parabola: $z=\\frac{1}{27}(y-1)^{2}$"] ["text" "Two intersecting lines: $y=\\pm 3(x+2)$"] ["text" "Two parallel lines: $x=3(z\\pm 1)$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-35577" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "A parabola: $z=\\frac{1}{27}(y-1)^{2}$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-164282" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $4(x-5)^{2}+6(y+4)^{2}=z+8$ and the plane \n$z=4$  onto the $xy$-plane is an ellipse. What are the lengths of the semi-axes of the ellipse?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-164282" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the intersection of the paraboloid and the plane $z=4$, we substitute $z=4$ into the equation of the paraboloid, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x-5)^{2}+6(y+4)^{2}=z+8 \\\\ 4(x-5)^{2}+6(y+4)^{2}=4+8 \\\\ 4(x-5)^{2}+6(y+4)^{2}=12 \\\\ \\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}+\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an ellipse with semi-axes of lengths $\\sqrt{3}$ and $\\sqrt{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-164282" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "908a1ded9102a559411c5aaef5ec93dd5c34a8a0a53ff518afe5707837569617"} {"question" "q-164282" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-164282" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$2\\sqrt{2}$ and $1$"] ["text" "$3$ and $2$"] ["text" "$9$ and $4$"] ["text" "$\\sqrt{3}$ and $1$"] ["text" "$\\sqrt{3}$ and $\\sqrt{2}$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-164282" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$\\sqrt{3}$ and $\\sqrt{2}$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-35906" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The projection of the intersection between the hyperbolic paraboloid $\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=z+2$ and the plane \n$y=3$  onto the $xz$-plane is a parabola. What is the vertex of this parabola?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-35906" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the intersection of the paraboloid and the plane $y=3$, we substitute $y=3$ into the equation of the paraboloid, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =z+2 \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{3}^{2}}{9} & =z+2 \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}-1 & =z+2 \\\\ z+3 & =\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4} \\\\ z & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+1)^{2}-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of a parabola in the $xz$-plane that has its vertex at $(-1,-3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-35906" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "908a1ded9102a559411c5aaef5ec93dd5c34a8a0a53ff518afe5707837569617"} {"question" "q-35906" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-35906" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$(-1,-3)$ in the $xz$-plane"] ["text" "$(-1,3)$ in the $xz$-plane"] ["text" "$(0,0)$ in the $xz$-plane"] ["text" "$(1,-3)$ in the $xz$-plane"] ["text" "$(1,3)$ in the $xz$-plane"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-35906" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$(-1,-3)$ in the $xz$-plane"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-35930" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $5(x+2)^{2}+3(y+3)^{2}=z$ and the plane $z=2$  onto the $xy$-plane is an ellipse. Find the center of this ellipse." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-35930" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the intersection of the paraboloid and the plane $z=2$, we substitute $z=2$ into the equation of the paraboloid, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(x+2)^{2}+3(y+3)^{2} & =z \\\\ 5(x+2)^{2}+3(y+3)^{2} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{2/5}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{2/3} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an ellipse in the $xy$-plane that has its center at $(-2,-3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-35930" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "908a1ded9102a559411c5aaef5ec93dd5c34a8a0a53ff518afe5707837569617"} {"question" "q-35930" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-35930" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$(-2,-3)$ in the $xy$-plane"] ["text" "$(-2,3)$ in the $xy$-plane"] ["text" "$(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane"] ["text" "$(2,-3)$ in the $xy$-plane"] ["text" "$(2,3)$ in the $xy$-plane"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "q-35930" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$(-2,-3)$ in the $xy$-plane"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/state.json" "file_sha256" "678b1750b593fdbd8d9ebb0744d4e1c19585a33ffc5f60dcf18c47bc45a8d1a7"} {"question" "e-5266" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the vertex and the axis of symmetry of the elliptic paraboloid $z= 4x^2 + 8(y + 2)^2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/example-5266.json" "file_sha256" "371246a380ed5583b3496a1ac61769e332358a2e8126ce6357270f97baf67c83"} {"question" "e-5266" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The general equation of an elliptic paraboloid oriented along the $z$-axis with vertex at a point $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$ is \n\n$$\nz - z_0 = \\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2}.\n$$\n\nTo find the vertex, we rewrite our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-0 & =4{x}^{2}+8(y+2)^{2} \\\\ z-0 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1/4}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{1/8} \\\\ z-0 & =\\frac{(x-0)^{2}}{1/4}+\\frac{(y-(-2))^{2}}{1/8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an elliptic paraboloid oriented along the $z$-axis that has its vertex at $(0,-2,0).$  Since it is oriented along the $z$-axis, we conclude that its axis of symmetry is parallel to the $z$-axis.\n\nThe axis of symmetry should pass through the vertex of the paraboloid. Therefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry can be written as an intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/example-5266.json" "file_sha256" "371246a380ed5583b3496a1ac61769e332358a2e8126ce6357270f97baf67c83"} {"question" "e-5269" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the saddle point of the hyperbolic paraboloid $z+1=\\dfrac{(x-1)^2}{4} - (y-2) ^2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/example-5269.json" "file_sha256" "03bbeada17fdcc9d46cdf6a2adc72b37a18ff0cc3f379f81449d46fdf2468454"} {"question" "e-5269" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The general equation of a hyperbolic paraboloid with a saddle point at $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$ is given by\n\n$$\nz - z_0 = \\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2}.\n$$\n\nTo find the saddle point, we rewrite our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+1 & =\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{4}-(y-2)^{2} \\\\ z-(-1) & =\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{4}-(y-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of a hyperbolic paraboloid with a saddle point at $(1,2,-1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/example-5269.json" "file_sha256" "03bbeada17fdcc9d46cdf6a2adc72b37a18ff0cc3f379f81449d46fdf2468454"} {"question" "e-6087" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The projection of the intersection between the hyperbolic paraboloid \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{4} - \\dfrac{(y+5) ^2}{3} = z-1\n$$\n\nand the plane $x=2$  onto the $yz$-plane is a parabola. Find the vertex of this parabola." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/example-6087.json" "file_sha256" "5049d2fa127602a99a757cb9cf8fa3a1affe68857400607b75035f10c6ac567a"} {"question" "e-6087" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the intersection of the paraboloid and the plane $x=2,$ we substitute $x=2$ into the equation of the paraboloid and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{3} & =z-1 \\\\ \\frac{{2}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{3} & =z-1 \\\\ 1-\\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{3} & =z-1 \\\\ z & =-\\frac{1}{3}(y+5)^{2}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nProjecting this onto the $yz$-plane, we get the equation of a parabola that has its vertex at $(-5,2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042526/example-6087.json" "file_sha256" "5049d2fa127602a99a757cb9cf8fa3a1affe68857400607b75035f10c6ac567a"}] "usage" {"q-73856" [] "e-5269" [] "e-6087" [] "e-5266" []} "basis" 1557 "retractions" [[17592186125134 :question/problem "Consider the elliptic paraboloid $z = ((x + 1)^{2})/(2) + ((y - 2)^{2})/(4)$. Which of the following gives its axis of symmetry, written as an intersection of two planes?"] [17592186124228 :question/problem "Find the vertex and the axis of symmetry of the elliptic paraboloid $z= 4x^2 + 8(y + 2)^2$."] [17592186124228 :question/worked-solution "The general equation of an elliptic paraboloid with vertex at a point $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$ is\n\n$$\nz - z_0 = \\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2}\n$$\n\nTo find the vertex, we rewrite our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz - 0 &= 4x^{2} + 8(y + 2)^{2} \\\\\nz - 0 &= \\frac{x^{2}}{1/4} + ((y + 2)^{2})/(1/8) \\\\\nz - 0 &= ((x - 0)^{2})/(1/4) + ((y - (-2))^{2})/(1/8)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an elliptic paraboloid that has its vertex at $(0,-2,0)$. Also, from the equation, we conclude that its axis of symmetry is parallel to the $z$-axis.\n\nThe axis of symmetry should pass through the vertex of the paraboloid. Therefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry can be written as an intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n{x = 0; y =-2\n$$"] [17592186124229 :question/problem "Find the saddle point of the hyperbolic paraboloid $z+1=\\dfrac{(x-1)^2}{4} - (y-2) ^2$."] [17592186124229 :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a hyperbolic paraboloid with a saddle point at $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$ is given by\n\n$$\nz - z_0 = \\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2}\n$$\n\nTo find the saddle point, we rewrite our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz + 1 &= ((x - 1)^{2})/(4) - (y - 2)^{2} \\\\\nz - (-1) &= ((x - 1)^{2})/(4) - (y - 2)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of a hyperbolic paraboloid with a saddle point at $(1,2,-1)$."] [17592186124283 :question/problem "The projection of the intersection between the hyperbolic paraboloid\n$\\dfrac{x^2}{4} - \\dfrac{(y+5) ^2}{3} = z-1$\nand the plane $x=2$ onto the $yz$-plane is a parabola. Find the vertex of this parabola."] [17592186124283 :question/worked-solution "To find the intersection of the paraboloid and the plane $x=2$, we substitute $x=2$ into the equation of the paraboloid and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x^{2}}{4} - ((y + 5)^{2})/(3) &= z - 1 \\\\\n\\frac{2^{2}}{4} - ((y + 5)^{2})/(3) &= z - 1 \\\\\n1 - ((y + 5)^{2})/(3) &= z - 1 \\\\\nz &= -\\frac{1}{3}(y + 5)^{2} + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nProjecting this onto the $yz$-plane, we get the equation of a parabola that has its vertex at $(-5,2)$."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186123827 17592186123828 17592186123829 17592186123830 17592186123831] "immutable_fields" [[17592186123826 :answer-field/correct] [17592186123826 :answer-field/id] [17592186123826 :answer-field/key] [17592186123826 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
